  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 311 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\n08z/2am surface analysis shows a 998mb low over northern\nOntario with a weak cold front/trough axis extending\nsouthwestward into the Upper Midwest. A band of high clouds\nassociated with this feature will stream into central Illinois\ntoday: however, the thin cloud cover will do little to mitigate\nwarming courtesy of W/SW winds of 10-15mph. Afternoon high\ntemperatures will top out in the lower 60s. Once the boundary \npasses, winds will become NW and decrease substantially after \nsunset. High pressure will build into the region and should be\npositioned directly overhead by 12z Mon...resulting in good\nradiational cooling conditions and early morning lows bottoming\nout in the lower to middle 30s. Fog development will be possible\nas well, although confidence remains low at this point. The RRFS\ncontinues to suggest widespread visibility restrictions by dawn\nMonday, while the HRRR/RAP focus pockets of fog primarily\nalong/west of I-55. Meanwhile the 00z NBM features just a 20-25%\nchance of visibilities less than 5 miles. Since it has not\nrained recently and the ground is dry, am skeptical widespread\nfog will be able to form even under ideal radiational cooling \nconditions. The more likely scenario would be fog along rivers\nand streams and in a few low-lying rural spots. Have therefore\nopted to leave fog mention out of the forecast until stronger\nevidence for impactful visby reductions materializes.  \n\nWith zonal flow in place across the CONUS, temperatures will\ncontinue to moderate as readings climb well into the 60s for\nboth Monday and Tuesday. After that, a short-wave trough\nejecting eastward from the Rockies and another wave diving \nsouthward out of Canada will phase over the Midwest/Great Lakes,\neventually forming a significant long-wave trough east of the\nMississippi River by the end of the week. GFS precipitable water\nvalues have risen from previous runs, with the 00z Nov 8\niteration now showing 1-1.25 ahead of the phasing system. Given\nstrong upper dynamics and an uptick in moisture, the latest NBM\nhas increased PoPs to 20-30 by Wednesday afternoon. All models\nshow the deep trough shifting east of Illinois by Thursday:\nhowever, a strong fetch of colder air flowing over the warmer\nwaters of Lake Michigan will lead to one or more bands of lake-\neffect showers Thursday and Thursday night. Based on the initial\nN/NE flow trajectory, some of the showers could potentially\nimpact locations east of I-57 on Thursday before shifting\nfurther E/NE by Thursday night into Friday. The main weather\nstory for the end of the week will be the temporary shot of much\ncooler air. The chilliest day will be Friday when highs only\nreach the middle to upper 40s. Once the trough shifts further\neastward and upper heights once again begin to rise, temps will\nclimb back into the lower 60s by next Sunday.",
